[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
I verified end-to-end reachability and routing state across the topology. The two user endpoints (pc0 at 20.1.1.10 and pc12 at 120.1.1.10) can reach each other bidirectionally with 0% loss and sub‑millisecond RTTs, confirming traffic successfully crosses the “far end” path (pc0 → r1 → OSPF core → r11 → EIGRP domain → r12 → pc12). OSPF is running and converged on all OSPF routers that should participate (r1–r10), with stable SPF timers and adjacency counts; the ABRs and virtual links between areas 0/1/2/3/4 are up (e.g., r4 and r8 each report one full virtual adjacency), so there is no backbone discontinuity. Route tables on core/edge routers (r1, r2, r3, r9, r10) show consistent OSPF internal routes and reception of external routes redistributed at r11 from EIGRP; r12/r13 handle the EIGRP side as expected. These observations do not show any routing loop or blackhole consistent with “an area summarizing itself into a loop.” If an earlier transient existed, it has cleared; current evidence indicates a healthy control plane and data plane.

Root cause: No active fault found; the network is presently forwarding correctly.

Corrective fix: No change required. If the issue recurs intermittently, capture “show ip route <dest>” and “show ip ospf database” on r1/r11 during the event, and consider tightening OSPF/EIGRP redistribution on r11 with route‑maps/tags to preclude any feedback under failure conditions.
=== END ===